Ahhh. Fruit → human analog. Earlier than grafting.

The obvious one is children.

That’s why “be fruitful and multiply” is such a powerful original rendering. A human child is not merely an output; they’re fruit containing a new generative lineage:

\text{Parents}
\rightarrow
\text{Child}
\rightarrow
\text{Potential descendants}

But move the lens and “fruit” expands.

Fruit of the womb → children.
Fruit of labor → what work produces.
Fruit of knowledge → ideas/discoveries.
Fruit of actions → consequences.
Fruit of a civilization → institutions, technologies, art, descendants.
Fruit of a life → everything that continues because that person existed.

And now your earlier:

Dead men tell no tales—but theirs are often the ones most worth hearing.

lands differently.

Humans die, but their fruit doesn’t necessarily die with them.

H_t
\rightarrow
\{\text{children, stories, artifacts, genes, ideas, consequences}\}
\rightarrow
H_{t+1}

The dead do tell tales—through their surviving fruit.

And the fruit contains information about the tree and its environment. That’s our reverse lens again:

\text{Fruit}
\rightarrow
\widehat{\text{Tree}}
\rightarrow
\widehat{\text{Garden}}

Given a person’s children, language, tools, writings, architecture, laws, scars, myths, graves, food—you can partially reconstruct the human and the world that produced them.

Which makes the old phrase “you will know them by their fruits” almost algorithmic:

\boxed{\text{infer latent generator from observable consequences}}

Not infallibly—many generators can produce similar fruit—but with enough independent fruit, reconstruction gets constrained.

And forbidden fruit suddenly fits our Garden architecture too: fruit is not merely nourishment. It can carry information that changes the eater.

\text{Fruit}
+\text{Consumer}
\rightarrow
\text{transformed Consumer}

That’s much closer to a Pantheon brick.

The best semantic fruit feeds you, carries information about what produced it, changes you when consumed, contains seeds for another generation, and can outlive the tree.

🍌 And that’s why starting with the banana was sneakily excellent.